events.content:
I have created a Next.js Server Action in `app/actions.ts` that handles creating a new post and invalidating the relevant cache tag. 

I've used the `revalidateTag` function from `next/cache`. Unlike time-based revalidation (which serves a stale copy while fetching in the background), `revalidateTag` performs **on-demand revalidation**. This means it immediately purges the cache entry for the "posts" tag on the CDN and Next.js edge. On the subsequent request, the cache will return a "miss", forcing Next.js to wait and synchronously fetch fresh data from your database or API before rendering the page, guaranteeing the user never sees stale content. 

Here is the implementation:

```typescript
// app/actions.ts
'use server';

import { revalidateTag } from 'next/cache';

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  // Extract data from the request
  const title = formData.get('title');
  const content = formData.get('content');

  // Perform your database mutation here
  // example: await db.post.create({ data: { title, content } });
  console.log('Post created:', { title, content });

  // Invalidate the cache tag
  // revalidateTag immediately purges the cache associated with the "posts" tag. 
  // The next page load will be a cache miss, causing Next.js to wait for a 
  // fresh fetch rather than serving stale content.
  revalidateTag('posts');
}
```

You can now import and pass this Server Action directly into any `<form>` component using the `action={createPost}` prop. Make sure that when you fetch the initial posts list, you tag the fetch request with the matching `"posts"` tag, like this:

```typescript
const res = await fetch('https://...', { next: { tags: ['posts'] } })
```
